{ ===========================================================================
  Psycho Neurosis (Asphyxia, 1994) -- NEUROSIS.001, scene 2, "ASPHYXIA PRESENTS"

  Split out of PART1_INTRO.PAS so this scene can be compiled and run on its
  own. Markers: [transcribed] read out of the binary, [inferred] implied by
  its call sites, [stub] signature only.
  =========================================================================== }

unit P1S2;

{$I-}   { NO I/O CHECK, and this unit had no I directive at all so it took the
          command line's $I+ default -- four five-byte near calls to the
          runtime's check routine that the original does not have. Part 004's
          Lemmings unit and part 006's P6S4 were missed the same way; a unit
          with no I directive at all is worth suspecting on sight. }

{$G+}   { 286: Title_Load 1082:0000 opens ENTER $182; every framed routine
          in the segment does. }

{$N+}   { 80x87 CODE ON. The original's segment 1082 carries 10 emulator
          traps -- INT $34..$3E, which is how an 80x87 instruction ships
          under $E+ -- and this unit had no N directive at all, so it took
          the command line's default and emitted NONE. $N- generates no
          80x87 instructions and therefore no traps either: floating point
          becomes far calls into the software six-byte-Real library, so a
          trap is positive evidence of $N+ rather than against it. Counted
          in 1082:0128's clipped blit region. Nothing here gains a coprocessor --
          Turbo Pascal's emulator ran these in 1994 and runs them now. }

interface

procedure Scene2;

implementation

uses VGA, Crt, DemoVT;

{$I gen/DATOFS.INC}   { NEUROSIS.DAT offsets and lengths, generated by
                        MKDAT from assets/NEUROSIS.MAN. Every constant in
                        it is UNTYPED, so including the whole set costs
                        no storage and changes no data image. }

const
  { 1082:00BB and 1082:05BB both pass 10 -- the delay between fade frames. }
  FadePause   = 10;
  CometFrames = 10;
  CometW      = 65;
  CometH      = 48;
  CometSize   = CometW * CometH;   { 3120 -- the stride in the loaded buffer }
  { 294, NOT 614, AND OURS WAS ADDING THE ROW TWICE. 1082:01d7 is MOV DI,$126 --
    294 -- and 1082:01da adds 320 on top of it, so the address DrawRevealed
    starts from is 614. Written as 614 the constant already carried the row and
    the ADD put a second one on: DI came out 934, a whole row and a bit past
    where the original writes. One byte in the image and a visibly wrong
    destination in the scene. }
  RevealOfs   = 294;               { $126, and 01da adds a row to it }
  FontBias    = 8209;              { = 32*256 + 17, see DrawChar }

const
  { TYPED, so they live in DGROUP where the original reads them from. The four
    addresses -- DS:$0004, $0006, $0008, $000A -- are the whole argument: an
    untyped const folds into the instruction and occupies no data at all, and
    part 001's initialised region opened eight bytes short because of it. }
  ClipX1 : Integer = 0;      { DS:$0004 }
  ClipX2 : Integer = 319;    { DS:$0006 }
  ClipY1 : Integer = 0;      { DS:$0008 }
  ClipY2 : Integer = 199;    { DS:$000A }

type
  { TWO-DIMENSIONAL, AND THE FOLDED -$1B IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT. DrawChar writes
    it at 1082:0249 and 02a3 as LES DI,[$56CE] / ADD DI,AX / ADD DI,DX /
    MOV ES:[DI-$1B],$79 -- TWO register terms added to the pointer and the
    constant folded into the displacement. A one-dimensional array folds
    nothing, so the -27 has to be computed, which is what ours did: it summed
    the row term, the column term and -27 into AX before touching DI.

    For array[1..R, 1..26] element (i, j) sits at base + i*26 + j - 27, and
    27 is exactly 1*26 + 1 -- the two low bounds. 200 rows of 26 is 5,200,
    which is the size this was declared flat as. The blur does not care: it is
    assembler and walks the buffer as bytes off LES DI. }
  TWorkBuf   = array[1..200, 1..26] of Byte;
  { ONE-BASED AND TWO-DIMENSIONAL, and the fold at the blit call says so.
    1082:0396 is IMUL AX,AX,$C30 -- the frame number times 3,120 -- and then
    1082:03a0 is ADD DI,$F3D0, which as a signed word is MINUS 3,120: one
    element's worth, folded into the address, which is what a 1-based array of
    3,120-byte frames gives. A flat array indexed (Frame - 1) * CometSize makes
    the compiler subtract the one at RUN TIME instead. Same 31,200 bytes. }
  TCometFrame = array[0..CometSize - 1] of Byte;
  TCometBank = array[1..CometFrames] of TCometFrame;
  { THREE-DIMENSIONAL, AND FontBias IS THE PROOF -- it was sitting in this
    file as a named constant for days. 1082:022b reaches a glyph byte as
    ES:[DI-$2011] with DI built from Ch shifted left 8, Phase shifted left 4 and
    C added, and $2011 is 8,209 = 32*256 + 1*16 + 1: the three low bounds folded
    into one displacement. Subtracting the same 8,209 by hand in a flat index
    makes the compiler do at RUN TIME what the type does at compile time. Part
    006's scene 1 font is the identical shape. Same 15,104 bytes: 59 glyphs from
    space to 'Z' of 16 rows of 16 columns. }
  TFontBuf   = array[#32..#90, 1..16, 1..16] of Byte;

var
  { THE ORDER BELOW IS THE ADDRESSES', and it was not before -- WorkBuf came
    first here and belongs fifth. IT IS RIGHT NOW, MEASURED: every absolute
    displacement into this block is identical in both images, with identical
    reference counts -- $56C0 three times, $56C4 twice, $56C8 once, $56CA four
    times, $56CE five times. dsmap reports six sites here at +10, +4 and -14,
    and those are MIS-PAIRINGS, not defects: it anchors on the bytes before a
    reference and this block has several that look alike, which is why it also
    reports 87 sites skipped as ambiguous. Read the displacements directly
    before believing a shift row in a block this dense.

    Sorted, this unit's block TILES with no gap:

        $56C0  CometBank      4
        $56C4  Backdrop       4
        $56C8  BackdropSeg    2
        $56CA  FontBuf        4
        $56CE  WorkBuf        4
        $56D2  Pal          768
        $59D2  F            128   -> $5A52

    All four pointers are HEAP blocks in the original -- Title_Load GetMem's
    5,200, 64,000, 31,200 and 15,104 bytes in that order -- so only their four
    bytes each live here. }
  CometBank   : ^TCometBank;    { DS:$56C0 -- 10 frames of 65 x 48 }
  Backdrop    : Pointer;        { DS:$56C4, segment kept at DS:$56C8 }
  BackdropSeg : Word;           { DS:$56C8 }
  FontBuf     : ^TFontBuf;      { DS:$56CA -- 59 glyphs of 16 x 16 }
  WorkBuf     : ^TWorkBuf;      { DS:$56CE }
  { DS:$56D2 -- THIS SCENE'S OWN PALETTE BUFFER, and it was missing entirely.
    1082:003f is MOV DI,$56D2 / PUSH $300 / BlockRead, so the 768 bytes are
    read into a unit-level buffer here and not into VGA's global. Part 003's
    sprite scene already records the same thing about itself. }
  Pal         : array[0..767] of Byte;  { DS:$56D2 }
  { DS:$59D2 -- THE FILE RECORD IS A GLOBAL. 1082:003a pushes a DS address to
    Assign where a local would be LEA'd off SS, and Load's frame confirms it:
    ENTER $182 is 386 bytes and references only [BP-2], with no room for a
    128-byte file record among the 384 that follow. }
  F           : file;           { DS:$59D2 }

{ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Title_Load (1082:0000)

  This was missing entirely, which is why the scene hung: WorkBuf was nil and
  BlurBuffer WRITES through it -- 3,900 bytes straight over the interrupt
  vector table at 0000:xxxx.

  NEUROSIS.DAT $00FD00, 111,072 bytes in four reads:

      768     palette
      31,200  the comet, 10 frames of 65 x 48 (10 * 3120)
      64,000  the backdrop screen
      15,104  the font, 59 glyphs of 16 x 16 (59 * 256)

  Between the screen read and the font read it blanks all 256 DAC entries,
  shows the screen, FADES UP to the palette it just loaded, and only then
  lays a RED ramp over entries 1..121 (R := I div 2) and shows it again.

  THE FADE-UP IS 1491:0215, a 64-step walk with Delay(10) between frames.
  An earlier version of this file did a plain SetPalette here, so the
  backdrop appeared instantly instead of coming up out of black. The
  matching fade-DOWN at the end of the scene was missing too -- see Scene2.

  The font block is GetMem'd LATE, after the fade, which is the original's
  order (1082:03A0) and is why it is not with the other three.
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------- }
procedure Load;
var
  { ENTER $182 IS 386 BYTES AND ONLY [BP-2] IS EVER REFERENCED, in the whole
    routine. So one 2-byte local is declared first and 384 bytes after it are
    never read -- and the file record is NOT among them, because Assign takes a
    DS address at 1082:003a where a local would be LEA'd off SS.

    2 + 256 + 128 = 386. The sizes are forced; the types are a guess, and two
    strings are chosen because P1S4's and P1S5's loaders have unreferenced
    string locals in exactly this position. }
  I      : Integer;                     { [BP-2] }
  Spare1 : String;                      { 256, never read }
  Spare2 : String[127];                 { 128, never read }
begin
  GetMem(WorkBuf,   SizeOf(TWorkBuf));        { 1082:0000 }
  GetMem(Backdrop,  64000);
  BackdropSeg := Seg(Backdrop^);
  GetMem(CometBank, SizeOf(TCometBank));

  { THE FILE IS OPENED BY THE CALLER, not here. 1082:0572..0597 is Assign,
    Reset and Seek in Scene2's own body, and 1082:059c is the near call to this
    routine afterwards -- so Load is entered with the file already positioned. }
  BlockRead(F, Pal, 768);                         { 1082:004b -> DS:$56D2 }
  BlockRead(F, CometBank^, SizeOf(TCometBank));
  ClearScreen($A000, 0);
  { THROUGH THE POINTER, NOT THROUGH THE SEGMENT. 1082:0076 is
    LES DI,[VirtScr] / PUSH ES / PUSH DI -- the far pointer loaded whole --
    where Mem[VirtScrSeg : 0] pushes the segment word and a zero offset and is
    a different instruction, not a different displacement. }
  BlockRead(F, VirtScr^, 64000);                  { 1082:0076 }

  { Blank, show, then fade UP to the palette just loaded. Only after that
    does the red ramp go over entries 1..121.

    An earlier version blanked the DAC and set nothing but the ramp, which
    left colours 122..255 BLACK. Most of the backdrop is in that range, so the
    scene ran correctly and displayed nothing. }
  for I := 0 to 255 do SetRGB(I, 0, 0, 0);        { 1082:0086 }
  CopyScreen(VirtScrSeg, $A000);                  { 1082:00B0 }
  PaletteFadeUp(@Pal, FadePause);                 { 1082:00b8, DS:$56D2 }
  for I := 1 to 121 do SetRGB(I, I div 2, 0, 0);  { 1082:00C6, the red ramp }
  CopyScreen(VirtScrSeg, $A000);
  CopyScreen(VirtScrSeg, BackdropSeg);            { keep a clean copy }

  GetMem(FontBuf, SizeOf(TFontBuf));              { 1082:03A0 -- late }
  BlockRead(F, FontBuf^, SizeOf(TFontBuf));
  { Close is the CALLER's, at 1082:05a4, after this routine returns. }
end;

{ [transcribed] 1082:01d0 -- redraws the characters revealed so far.

  A 150-row by 26-column masked blit out of the working buffer at DS:$56CE.
  The mask is a THRESHOLD, not a key: source bytes of 20 or less are skipped,
  so the anti-aliased edges of the glyphs drop out and only the solid core is
  copied. }
{ @asm 001 1082:0128 +0 BlitClipped }
procedure BlitClipped(Src : Pointer; X, Y : Integer; Segment : Word); assembler;
var
  SkipL : Integer;                { [BP-$02] }
  SkipR : Integer;                { [BP-$04] }
  Spare : Integer;                { [BP-$06], never touched }
asm
        PUSH    DS                      { 1082:012c                          }
        MOV     AX, Segment             { 012d                               }
        MOV     ES, AX                  { 0130  ES:DI is the screen          }
        LDS     SI, Src                 { 0132  DS:SI is the sprite...       }
        XOR     DH, DH                  { 0135                               }
        MOV     DL, CometH              { 0137  DX := 48 rows                }
        XOR     CH, CH                  { 0139                               }
        MOV     CL, CometW              { 013b  CX := 65 columns             }
        POP     DS                      { 013d  ...but DGROUP again for now, }
        MOV     WORD PTR SkipL, 0       { 013e    because the clip bounds    }
        MOV     WORD PTR SkipR, 0       { 0143    are read out of it         }
        MOV     BX, Y                   { 0148                               }
        MOV     AX, ClipY1              { 014b                               }
        SUB     AX, BX                  { 014e                               }
        JLE     @@NoTop                 { 0150                               }
        CMP     AX, DX                  { 0152                               }
        JGE     @@Done                  { 0154  wholly above the window      }
        SUB     DX, AX                  { 0156                               }
        ADD     BX, AX                  { 0158                               }
        MOV     DI, DX                  { 015a  park the row count round MUL }
        MUL     CX                      { 015c  clipped rows x 65            }
        MOV     DX, DI                  { 015e                               }
        ADD     SI, AX                  { 0160                               }
@@NoTop:
        MOV     AX, ClipY2              { 0162                               }
        SUB     AX, BX                  { 0165                               }
        JLE     @@Done                  { 0167  wholly below it              }
        CMP     AX, DX                  { 0169                               }
        JGE     @@NoBot                 { 016b                               }
        INC     AX                      { 016d                               }
        MOV     DX, AX                  { 016e                               }
@@NoBot:
        ADD     BX, BX                  { 0170                               }
        MOV     DI, [BX + OFFSET YOfs]  { 0172  DI := Y * 320                }
        MOV     BX, X                   { 0176                               }
        ADD     DI, BX                  { 0179                               }
        MOV     AX, ClipX1              { 017b                               }
        SUB     AX, BX                  { 017e                               }
        JLE     @@NoLeft                { 0180                               }
        CMP     AX, CX                  { 0182                               }
        JGE     @@Done                  { 0184  wholly left of the window    }
        MOV     WORD PTR SkipL, AX      { 0186  skip this many per row       }
        ADD     DI, AX                  { 0189                               }
        SUB     CX, AX                  { 018b                               }
@@NoLeft:
        MOV     AX, ClipX2              { 018d                               }
        SUB     AX, BX                  { 0190                               }
        JLE     @@Done                  { 0192  wholly right of it           }
        CMP     AX, CX                  { 0194                               }
        JGE     @@NoRight               { 0196                               }
        SUB     CX, AX                  { 0198                               }
        MOV     WORD PTR SkipR, CX      { 019a                               }
        MOV     CX, AX                  { 019d                               }
@@NoRight:
        MOV     BX, CX                  { 019f  BX := the drawn width        }
        { DS IS RELOADED THE LONG WAY ROUND. LDS overwrites SI, and the SI it
          overwrites is the clipped start the code has just spent forty
          instructions computing -- so SI is pushed, the LDS is done for its DS
          alone, and SI is popped straight back. Four bytes and it is what the
          original does. }
        PUSH    DS                      { 01a1                               }
        PUSH    SI                      { 01a2                               }
        LDS     SI, Src                 { 01a3  for DS only                  }
        POP     SI                      { 01a6                               }
        CLD                             { 01a7                               }
@@Row:  ADD     SI, SkipL               { 01a8  <- once per row              }
@@Pixel:
        LODSB                           { 01ab                               }
        OR      AL, AL                  { 01ac                               }
        JE      @@Clear                 { 01ae                               }
        STOSB                           { 01b0                               }
        DEC     CL                      { 01b1                               }
        JNE     @@Pixel                 { 01b3                               }
        JMP     @@NextRow               { 01b5                               }
@@Clear:
        INC     DI                      { 01b7                               }
        DEC     CL                      { 01b8                               }
        JNE     @@Pixel                 { 01ba                               }
@@NextRow:
        ADD     SI, SkipR               { 01bc                               }
        MOV     CX, BX                  { 01bf  the width back for next row  }
        ADD     DI, 320                 { 01c1                               }
        SUB     DI, BX                  { 01c5                               }
        DEC     DL                      { 01c7                               }
        JNE     @@Row                   { 01c9                               }
        POP     DS                      { 01cb                               }
@@Done:                                 { 01cc is the LEAVE / RET $A         }
end;

{ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Title_ShowWord (1082:02fd)

  Called twice -- once with 'ASPHYXIA' (1082:$055E) and once with
  'PRESENTS' (1082:$0567). A counter runs from 80 down to -18 while a comet
  sprite flies across; as it passes, characters are revealed one at a time.
  When the counter reaches -18 a 100-frame outro plays.

  ASSETS  NEUROSIS.DAT $00FD00, 111,072 bytes:
            assets/part001/INTROPAL.PAL    768 B
            assets/part001/COMET0.PNG .. COMET9.PNG
                                      10 frames of 65x48 -- a flaming comet
            assets/part001/INTROBAK.PNG   64,000 B
            assets/part001/00FD00D.BIN        15,104 B = a 59-character
                                      16x16 font (59 x 256), drawn two
                                      rows at a time by 1082:0200
          A 5,200-byte working buffer is allocated separately.
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------- }

{ @asm 001 1082:01d0 +0 DrawRevealed }
procedure DrawRevealed; assembler;
asm
        PUSH    ES                      { 1082:01d0                          }
        PUSH    DS                      { 01d1                               }
        MOV     AX, [VirtScrSeg]        { 01d2                               }
        MOV     ES, AX                  { 01d5  ES:DI is the virtual screen   }
        MOV     DI, RevealOfs           { 01d7  $266                         }
        ADD     DI, 320                 { 01da  one row down to start         }
        LDS     SI, WorkBuf             { 01de  DS:SI is the working buffer   }
        MOV     BX, 150                 { 01e2  rows                         }
@@Row:  MOV     CX, 26                  { 01e5  columns, reloaded per row     }
@@Pixel:
        MOV     AL, [SI]                { 01e8                               }
        CMP     AL, 20                  { 01ea  THE MASK IS A THRESHOLD       }
        JLE     @@Skip                  { 01ec    and not a colour key        }
        MOV     ES:[DI], AL             { 01ee                               }
@@Skip: INC     DI                      { 01f1  BOTH advance either way, so   }
        INC     SI                      { 01f2    the skip is a hole and not  }
        DEC     CX                      { 01f3    a shift                     }
        JNE     @@Pixel                 { 01f4                               }
        ADD     DI, 292                 { 01f6  $124 -- and DI is already 26  }
        DEC     BX                      { 01fa    further on, so the stride   }
        JNE     @@Row                   { 01fb    is 318 and the block LEANS  }
        POP     DS                      { 01fd                               }
        POP     ES                      { 01fe                               }
end;
{ [stub] 1082:0200 -- draws the character currently animating. Phase is
  Trunc(Phase) from ShowWord: one of 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15. }
{ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1082:0200 -- draw one character into the work buffer.

  It does TWO font rows per call, Phase and Phase + 1, which is what makes the
  glyph unroll two scanlines at a time as Phase steps 1, 3, 5 .. 15.

  The font is 16 x 16: 256 bytes per glyph, 16 per row -- which is where the
  15,104-byte read comes from (59 * 256). The work buffer is 26 bytes per row,
  the same 26 that DrawRevealed blits and BlurBuffer smears.

      if Font[Ord(Ch)*256 + Phase*16 + C - 8209] <> 0 then
        Work[((Index-1)*17 + Phase + 1) * 26 + C + 5 - 27] := 121

  The -8209 is not decoration: the font holds 59 glyphs, so a raw Ord('A')*256
  would run 1,500 bytes off the end of the 15,104-byte buffer. 8209 = 32*256
  + 17, i.e. the table starts at space and each glyph is 17 bytes short of a
  full 256 -- which is what brings 'A'..'Z' back inside it.

  Every lit pixel is written as colour $79 = 121; there is no shading here,
  the gradient comes from BlurBuffer afterwards.
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------- }
{ ONE LOCAL, NOT THREE. 1082:0200 opens ENTER $02 and [BP-$02] is the only slot
  the routine names, so Row and Src were never variables -- both are expressions
  the compiler evaluates in place, and declaring them cost four bytes of frame
  and a store apiece. }
procedure DrawChar(Ch : Char; Phase, Index : Integer);
var
  C : Integer;                     { [BP-$02], the whole frame }
begin
  for C := 1 to 16 do              { 1082:0204 }
    if FontBuf^[Ch, Phase, C] <> 0 then                        { 1082:022b }
      WorkBuf^[(Index - 1) * 17 + Phase + 1, C + 5] := 121;   { 1082:0249 }
  for C := 1 to 16 do
    if FontBuf^[Ch, Phase + 1, C] <> 0 then
      WorkBuf^[(Index - 1) * 17 + Phase + 2, C + 5] := 121;   { 1082:02a3 }
end;

{ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1082:02ba -- NOT an outro. It is a BLUR.

  3,900 iterations of a diagonal four-neighbour average over the working
  buffer:

      P[0] := (P[-27] + P[-25] + P[+25] + P[+27]) shr 2

  With a row width of 26 those offsets are the four DIAGONAL neighbours, and
  3,900 is exactly 26 x 150 -- the same buffer DrawRevealed reads. Running it
  100 times is what dissolves the finished word into a smear rather than
  cutting away from it.

  The earlier name and body were guesses from the call site alone.
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------- }
{ HAND ASSEMBLER, TRANSCRIBED VERBATIM, and three things say so. 1082:02ba goes
  straight into LES DI with no prologue at all, so the routine has no frame and
  no locals; the counter is CX and the loop is LOOP; and the store is STOSB,
  which also advances DI, so the pointer walks itself and there is no index
  variable anywhere. Compiled Pascal emits none of the three for this shape.

  IT ALSO READS AND WRITES THE SAME BUFFER AS IT GOES. DI starts 27 bytes in and
  STOSB writes at DI before incrementing it, so the four neighbours are read
  relative to the byte about to be overwritten -- and the two ABOVE it
  ([DI-$1B] and [DI-$19]) have already been blurred by earlier iterations. It is
  a running smear, not a clean convolution, and that is what makes a hundred
  passes dissolve the word rather than merely soften it.

  EQUIVALENT PASCAL, for reference -- the assembler below is what runs:

      for I := 27 to 27 + 3899 do
        WorkBuf^[I] := (WorkBuf^[I - 27] + WorkBuf^[I - 25] +
                        WorkBuf^[I + 25] + WorkBuf^[I + 27]) shr 2;
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------- }
{ @asm 001 1082:02ba +0 BlurBuffer }
procedure BlurBuffer; assembler;
asm
        LES     DI, WorkBuf             { 1082:02ba  ES:DI := the buffer      }
        ADD     DI, 27                  { 02be  the first byte with four      }
                                        {         diagonal neighbours         }
        MOV     CX, 3900                { 02c1  26 x 150, the whole band      }
                                        {         loop, so BX is a widened BL }
@@Pixel:
        { THE LABEL COMES BEFORE THE TWO XORs, so AX and BX are re-zeroed
          every pixel. 1082:02e4's LOOP goes back to 02c4, which is the
          first XOR -- not to 02c8, the first MOV. Hoisting them out of the
          loop, which is what the comment about BH staying zero invited,
          moved the label four bytes and was the only difference left in
          this routine. }
        XOR     AX, AX                  { 02c4                                }
        XOR     BX, BX                  { 02c6  BH stays zero for the whole   }
        MOV     BL, ES:[DI-27]          { 02c8  up and left                   }
        MOV     AX, BX                  { 02cc                                }
        MOV     BL, ES:[DI-25]          { 02ce  up and right                  }
        ADD     AX, BX                  { 02d2                                }
        MOV     BL, ES:[DI+25]          { 02d4  down and left                 }
        ADD     AX, BX                  { 02d8                                }
        MOV     BL, ES:[DI+27]          { 02da  down and right                }
        ADD     AX, BX                  { 02de                                }
        SHR     AX, 2                   { 02e0  the average of the four       }
        STOSB                           { 02e3  and DI steps on by itself     }
        LOOP    @@Pixel                 { 02e4                                }
end;

{ ===========================================================================
  Scene 2 -- "ASPHYXIA PRESENTS"                  Demo_Scene2  1082:056f
  =========================================================================== }


{ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Sprite_BlitClipped (1082:0128) -- the comet. HAND ASSEMBLER, verbatim.

  A 65 x 48 transparent blit clipped against four bounds in DGROUP:

      DS:$0004  ClipX1 = 0      DS:$0006  ClipX2 = 319
      DS:$0008  ClipY1 = 0      DS:$000A  ClipY2 = 199

  The clip is done by adjusting the START POINTER and the row and column COUNTS
  before the copy, never per pixel: a top clip advances the source by
  (rows * 65) with a MUL, a left clip advances the destination and shortens the
  row, and both leftovers are added back between rows. Zero bytes are skipped.

  IT IS ASSEMBLER AND THE SIZE CONSTANTS ARE THE FIRST TELL. 1082:0137 is
  MOV DL,$30 and 1082:013b is MOV CL,$41 -- the height and width as IMMEDIATES
  into the halves of two registers, with XOR DH,DH and XOR CH,CH to clear the
  other halves. Compiled Pascal loads a Word constant whole. Add LODSB/STOSB for
  one byte at a time, DEC CL / JNE for the inner loop, and MUL CX to skip rows,
  and there is nothing of the code generator left in it. The sister blitters in
  scene 5 -- DrawPoint and DrawReflection -- are the same routine with the size
  read from the sprite instead of compiled in.

  TWO LOCALS AND A SPARE. ENTER $06, with [BP-$02] and [BP-$04] both zeroed at
  the top and written by the horizontal clip; nothing touches [BP-$06]. An
  `assembler` routine may declare locals and Turbo Pascal frames them, which is
  why the operand is there at all.

  EQUIVALENT PASCAL, for reference -- the assembler below is what runs:

      Rows := CometH;  Cols := CometW;  SkipL := 0;  SkipR := 0;
      if ClipY1 - Y > 0 then
      begin
        if ClipY1 - Y >= Rows then Exit;
        Dec(Rows, ClipY1 - Y);  Inc(P, (ClipY1 - Y) * CometW);  Y := ClipY1;
      end;
      if ClipY2 - Y <= 0 then Exit;
      if ClipY2 - Y < Rows then Rows := ClipY2 - Y + 1;
      D := YOfs[Y] + X;
      if ClipX1 - X > 0 then
      begin
        if ClipX1 - X >= Cols then Exit;
        SkipL := ClipX1 - X;  Inc(D, SkipL);  Dec(Cols, SkipL);
      end;
      if ClipX2 - X <= 0 then Exit;
      if ClipX2 - X < Cols then
      begin
        SkipR := Cols - (ClipX2 - X);  Cols := ClipX2 - X;
      end;
      for Row := 1 to Rows do
      begin
        Inc(P, SkipL);
        for Col := 1 to Cols do
        begin
          if P^ <> 0 then Mem[Segment : D] := P^;
          Inc(P);  Inc(D);
        end;
        Inc(P, SkipR);  Inc(D, 320 - Cols);
      end;
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------- }

{ --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Title_ShowWord (1082:02fd)

  Called twice -- once with 'ASPHYXIA' (1082:$055E) and once with
  'PRESENTS' (1082:$0567). A counter runs from 80 down to -18 while a comet
  sprite flies across; as it passes, characters are revealed one at a time.
  When the counter reaches -18 a 100-frame outro plays.

  ASSETS  NEUROSIS.DAT $00FD00, 111,072 bytes:
            assets/part001/INTROPAL.PAL    768 B
            assets/part001/COMET0.PNG .. COMET9.PNG
                                      10 frames of 65x48 -- a flaming comet
            assets/part001/INTROBAK.PNG   64,000 B
            assets/part001/00FD00D.BIN        15,104 B = a 59-character
                                      16x16 font (59 x 256), drawn two
                                      rows at a time by 1082:0200
          A 5,200-byte working buffer is allocated separately.
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------- }
procedure ShowWord(S : String);
{ Read instruction by instruction off 1082:02fd. Three of the four state
  variables are Borland Reals held in the stack frame, and the four constants
  are FP operands in the code segment -- decoded with tools/fpconst.py:

      CS:$02E7  single  2.0     added to Phase AND to CometX
      CS:$02EB  single 15.0     Phase wrap
      CS:$02EF  ext80   0.3     added to Frame
      CS:$02F9  single 10.0     Frame wrap

  and the initial Real48 values in the frame are CometPos = -48.0, Frame = 1.0,
  Phase = 1.0. Nothing here is fixed point -- this really is the FP scene. }
var
  { COUNT COMES FIRST, ahead of the three Reals, and the counter's own offset
    says so. 1082:0510 is CMP WORD PTR [BP-$102],-$12 -- the slot immediately
    below the 256-byte string parameter, so Count is the first local declared --
    where ours had it last and emitted [BP-$114]. Every Real moved two bytes
    with it: the original sets them at [BP-$10E] and [BP-$108] and ours at
    [BP-$10C] and [BP-$106]. The frame total is the same either way, ENTER $118,
    which is why prologue.py reads this routine as matching. }
  Count                  : Integer;   { [BP-$102] }
  CometPos, Frame, Phase : Real;      { [BP-$108], [BP-$10E], [BP-$114] }
  CharIdx                : Integer;   { [BP-$116] }
begin
  { CharIdx BEFORE Phase, WHICH READS ODDLY AND IS WHAT THE ORIGINAL DOES.
    1082:033c stores $FFFF into [BP-$116], CharIdx, and only then does
    1082:0342 begin the three words of Phase at [BP-$114]. Assignment order is
    emission order, so the natural grouping -- the three Reals together, the
    Integer after -- puts these six bytes in the wrong place. }
  CometPos := -48.0;
  Frame   :=   1.0;
  CharIdx :=  -1;
  Phase   :=   1.0;

  { 1082:0354 -- FillChar(WorkBuf, 5200, 0). ShowWord clears the work buffer
    on EVERY call, which is what stops the first word's blurred remains (and,
    on the first call, raw heap garbage) showing through. DrawRevealed stores
    any byte above 20, so without this there is a permanent stripe. }
  FillChar(WorkBuf^, SizeOf(TWorkBuf), 0);

  { A FOR ... DOWNTO, NOT A REPEAT WITH A Dec, AND THE TWO LOOPS IN THIS
    ROUTINE HAVE THE SAME SHAPE. 1082:0364 is MOV [BP-$102],$50 / JMP into the
    body / 1082:036c DEC [BP-$102], and the bottom at 1082:0510 is
    CMP [BP-$102],-$12 / JE out / JMP back to the DEC -- which is exactly what
    the outro's `for Count := 1 to 100` emits below with INC in place of DEC.
    The decrement belongs at the TOP, skipped on the first pass by that JMP.

    Written as `Count := 80; repeat ... Dec(Count) ... until Count = -18` the
    decrement lands at the bottom instead, which is four bytes there and six
    missing here, and the loop runs 98 times against the original's 99: a for
    loop's body runs for the limit value too, so the original's last pass has
    Count = -18 and ours stopped at -17. One frame of the comet, and it is the
    frame furthest off the left edge. }
  for Count := 80 downto -18 do
  begin
    CopyScreen(BackdropSeg, VirtScrSeg);
    DrawRevealed;                    { 1082:01d0 -- the characters so far }

    { The comet. The push order at 1082:03a6 settles which is which:
      Count*4 goes to [BP+8] = X and Trunc(CometPos) to [BP+6] = Y. So the
      COUNTER drives X and the accumulating Real drives Y -- the opposite of
      an earlier reading.

          X = Count * 4      320 down to -72     right to left
          Y = CometPos       -48  up to  148     top to bottom

      so it sweeps from the top right down to the bottom left. The bank index
      is (Frame - 1), which the original writes as +$F3D0, i.e. -CometSize. }
    { Round, NOT Trunc, AT ALL THREE SITES IN THIS LOOP -- the frame, the comet's
      Y and the glyph phase. Each one is a single far call into the runtime and
      the offsets differ: 1082:0392, 03c0 and 03fb call System:$3268 where Trunc
      compiles to $3249. Same length, one byte apart in the image, and a
      different answer whenever the value is not exact -- Frame steps by 0.3, so
      Trunc held each frame a step longer than the original does. Those three
      bytes were the last of this unit; it is now byte-for-byte identical to
      segment 1082. }
    BlitClipped(@CometBank^[Round(Frame)],       { 1082:0396 }
                Count * 4, Round(CometPos), VirtScrSeg);

    { Only the character currently animating is drawn here; Trunc(Phase) is
      its animation step, one of 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15. }
    { CharIdx < Length(S) + 1, AND NOT CharIdx <= Length(S). 1082:03d9 is
      INC AX on the length before the compare, and the branch is JLE where the
      shorter form gives JL -- so the original added one to the limit and used a
      strict less-than. The two are the same test on an Integer; only one of
      them costs the extra byte, and it is the one that is there. }
    if (CharIdx > 0) and (CharIdx < Length(S) + 1) then
      DrawChar(S[CharIdx], Round(Phase), CharIdx);

    Phase := Phase + 2.0;
    if Phase > 15.0 then
    begin
      Phase := 1.0;
      { LONGHAND. 1082:0466 is MOV AX,[BP-$116] / INC AX / MOV [BP-$116],AX --
        nine bytes -- where Inc emits one four-byte INC WORD PTR [mem]. Eighth
        time in this project. }
      CharIdx := CharIdx + 1;          { eight frames per character }
    end;

    CopyScreen(VirtScrSeg, $A000);

    Frame := Frame + 0.3;            { ~31 frames for the 10-frame cycle }
    if Frame > 10.0 then Frame := 1.0;

    CometPos := CometPos + 2.0;

    { 1082:050c -- the KeyPressed test has TWO destinations: the `je` falls
      through to the loop's own test, the `jmp` goes to 1082:054c, which is
      PAST the 100-frame outro below. So this is not part of the loop
      condition -- it is an Exit, and a key pressed during the word therefore
      skips the fade entirely. }
    if KeyPressed then Exit;
  end;

  { --------------------------------------------------------------------
    The outro, 1082:051a..054a -- 100 frames, and it REDRAWS every one:

        CopyScreen(Backdrop -> Virt)
        DrawRevealed                    (* the work buffer as it stands *)
        BlurBuffer                      (* 1082:02ba, one pass *)
        CopyScreen(Virt -> $A000)

    That is the fade. BlurBuffer alone changes nothing on screen; it is the
    re-blit each frame that makes the smear visible, and because DrawRevealed
    only stores bytes above 20, the letters stop being drawn at all once the
    blur has pulled them under that threshold. Running the 100 passes without
    redrawing -- which is what an earlier version did -- leaves the finished
    word sitting there and then cuts away.
    -------------------------------------------------------------------- }
  for Count := 1 to 100 do
  begin
    CopyScreen(BackdropSeg, VirtScrSeg);
    DrawRevealed;
    BlurBuffer;
    CopyScreen(VirtScrSeg, $A000);
  end;
end;



procedure Scene2;

begin
  { Opened HERE, before Load, which is where the original has it. }
  Assign(F, 'neurosis.dat');              { 1082:057c }
  Reset(F, 1);                            { 1082:0588 }
  Seek(F, DAT_P1_INTROPAL);               { 1082:0597 -- was $00FD00 }
  Load;                                   { 1082:059c, a near call to offset 0 }
  Close(F);                               { 1082:05a4, AFTER Load returns }
  ShowWord('ASPHYXIA');                   { 1082:0592 }
  ShowWord('PRESENTS');                   { 1082:059D }
  PaletteFadeOut(FadePause);              { 1082:05BB -- 1491:0330 }
  { THE FOUR FreeMems ARE INLINE, and the segment's arithmetic leaves nowhere
    else for them. 1082 runs Load 0000, BlitClipped 0128, DrawRevealed 01d0,
    DrawChar 0200, BlurBuffer 02ba, ShowWord 02fd, Scene2 056f and the
    initialisation 0602 -- contiguous, with no gap for an Unload routine. And
    1082:0601 is CB, a FAR return, which is Scene2's own epilogue; ours emitted
    C3 because a near local Unload ended there instead. }
  { THE ORDER IS CometBank, Backdrop, FontBuf, WorkBuf -- and the sizes pushed
    are what say so. 1082:05c8 pushes $79E0, 31,200, which is CometBank;
    1082:05d8 pushes $FA00, 64,000, the backdrop; 1082:05e8 pushes $3B00,
    15,104, the font. Ours freed the font FIRST, so all three calls named the
    wrong pointer and the wrong size: seventeen bytes, twelve runs, every one a
    DS displacement or a size immediate rotated by one place. }
  FreeMem(CometBank, SizeOf(TCometBank));  { 1082:05C1, $79E0 = 31,200 }
  FreeMem(Backdrop,  64000);               { 1082:05D1, $FA00 }
  FreeMem(FontBuf,   SizeOf(TFontBuf));    { 1082:05E1, $3B00 = 15,104 }
  FreeMem(WorkBuf,   SizeOf(TWorkBuf));    { 1082:05F1 }
end;

{ AN EMPTY INITIALISATION SECTION, AND THE ORIGINAL GIVES EVERY UNIT ONE. 1082:0602
  is PUSH BP / MOV BP,SP / LEAVE / RETF -- five bytes, doing nothing -- and the
  main body opens with a chain of far calls that runs one per unit. The chain is
  twelve calls at 1000:0000..003b in the 1994 binary and was SEVEN in ours,
  because the five scene units ended `end.` with no `begin`, so TP7 emitted no
  section and the chain had nothing to call. Twenty-five bytes of stub and
  twenty-five of chain, and the body started twenty-five bytes early.

  Without the `begin` below this unit does not appear in that chain at all. }
begin
end.
